# Variables read by apps/mobile.
#
# Copy this to `apps/mobile/.env`. **Not** the repository root .env, which is
# where every other application reads from — this one is different, and the
# difference was measured rather than assumed:
#
#   apps/mobile/.env          the values are compiled into the bundle
#   the root .env             not read by Expo
#   exported in your shell    not read either
#
# Expo substitutes EXPO_PUBLIC_* into the JavaScript at build time by looking in
# the project's own .env. A shell that exports the root .env — as this
# repository's does — makes the values visible to the `expo start` process and
# still does not put them in the bundle, which fails at launch with a
# configuration error naming the missing variable.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_ means public. A value compiled into a binary is discoverable
# (security.md:1655). Addresses belong here. A credential never does.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the backends are
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Two addresses because there are two backends: REST to the authoritative API,
# GraphQL to the read-only Timeline aggregator. Both must be reachable from the
# same place, because the app talks to both.
#
# Which host works depends on where the app is running, and this is the single
# most common reason a device cannot reach a machine that is plainly serving
# requests:
#
#   iOS simulator     localhost          — shares the host's network
#   Android emulator  10.0.2.2           — the emulator's alias for the host
#   Expo Go on a phone  your machine's LAN address, e.g. 192.168.1.24 — the
#                       phone is not the host, and localhost is the phone
#
# `expo start` prints the address it is serving on (exp://<address>:8081). That
# address is the one a phone can reach, so it is the right host to use here.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3000
EXPO_PUBLIC_FEED_URL=http://localhost:4000
